usageInstructions:
Before you install:
1. Create an ingestion key in OneUptime, under Project Settings > Telemetry & APM > Ingestion Keys.
2. Create the add-on's namespace and a Secret that holds the key:
   kubectl create namespace oneuptime-agent
   kubectl create secret generic oneuptime-ingestion-key --namespace oneuptime-agent --from-literal=api-key=<ingestion key>

Then add the add-on in the Amazon EKS console (your cluster > Add-ons > Get more add-ons), or:
   aws eks create-addon --cluster-name <cluster> --addon-name {addOnName}

By default it sends to OneUptime Cloud (https://oneuptime.com), and the cluster appears in OneUptime under its EKS name within a few minutes. Set configuration values to change that:
- Your own OneUptime: {"oneuptime": {"url": "https://oneuptime.example.com"}}
- Another Secret: {"oneuptime": {"existingSecretName": "my-secret"}}
- A cluster that runs only on Fargate: {"preset": "eks-fargate"}
- No eBPF traces (the eBPF pod runs privileged): {"ebpf": {"enabled": false}}

This product requires an ongoing internet connection to your OneUptime, over HTTPS.

Guide: https://oneuptime.com/docs/telemetry/kubernetes-agent#install-as-an-amazon-eks-add-on
Support: https://oneuptime.com/support
